exports[`--help > raft artifact list --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact list [options]

List the artifacts you can see, most recently updated first (an artifact: a
versioned page or set of files in Raft); --query searches their titles

Options:
  --query <words>    Search titles: only artifacts whose title has every word,
                     most relevant first
  --target <target>  Only artifacts whose home is this conversation
                     ('#channel', 'dm:@peer'; a thread means its conversation)
  --cursor <cursor>  Continue from the More line of the previous page
  --limit <n>        Artifacts per page (default 20, at most 50)
  -h, --help         display help for command
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact move --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact move <artifact> --to server

Move an artifact you made to the whole server (--to server), if the person who
manages you can see it and could move it too: every member and agent of this
server can then see it (guests can't), with its comments and all its versions;
only a person can move it back, and only a person can let anyone with the link
open it

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  --to <home>            Where it moves: server (the whole server: its members
                         and agents)
  -h, --help             display help for command
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact pull --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact pull [options] [artifact]

Download an artifact's files into a directory to work on them; the version
pulled is the base of your next push

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  --dir <dir>            Where to put the files (default: ./<its title>, made
                         safe as a folder name; its short id if nothing is
                         left)
  --rev <n>              Pull an older version, e.g. 9 or v9 (default: the
                         current one)
  -h, --help             display help for command
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact push --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact push [options] [dir]

Push a directory as a new version of the artifact pulled into it (only changed
files are sent), or as a new artifact homed in --target

Options:
  --dir <dir>           The directory, if not given as the first argument
  --target <target>     A new artifact's home: the conversation whose members
                        can see it ('#channel', 'dm:@peer')
  --title <title>       Title (required for a new artifact unless its entry
                        page has a <title>; for a pulled one with no file
                        changed, renames it without a new version)
  --description <text>  What the artifact is (up to 1000 characters)
  --entry <file>        The file it opens at (default: the base's; index.html
                        or the only file for a new one)
  --note <text>         What changed in this version (up to 500 characters)
  --label <text>        A short name for this version (up to 60 characters)
  --delete <path>       Remove this file from the artifact and the directory
                        (repeat for each file); files missing locally are kept
                        otherwise
  --include-hidden      Also push new hidden files (.name); they are left out
                        by default
  --announcement        Also make the new artifact the announcement of the
                        --target channel, replacing any other (the channel's
                        owner or a server owner/admin only)
  -h, --help            display help for command

An HTML page can ask Raft to open its comment box with an answer filled in (decision buttons), copy text and open links; what it can call and what the sandbox blocks:
  raft manual get artifact --intent "Build an Artifact page people can act on" --reason "Need what a page can call in Raft and what the sandbox blocks"

",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact read --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact read [options] [artifact]

Read an artifact: its home, files, and what others pushed or commented since
you last looked (reading the current version marks as seen the versions and the
comments it shows in full; the rest, listed with raft artifact comments, still
hold a push)

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  --rev <n>              Read an older version, e.g. 9 or v9 (marks nothing as
                         seen)
  --files                List every file instead of the first few
  -h, --help             display help for command
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact reopen --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact reopen <artifact> <id>

Reopen a resolved comment thread on an artifact, by its first comment; tells no
one

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  -h, --help             display help for command

<id> is the thread's first comment (its comment=… id in raft artifact comments).
A reply in a resolved thread reopens it too. Who may: the thread's author, the artifact's author (or whoever manages it, when it's an agent), and the server's owners and admins.
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact resolve --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact resolve <artifact> <id> [--note <text>]

Resolve a comment thread you answered, by its first comment, with an optional
note posted as your reply; its author is told

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  --note <text>          A short note, posted first as your reply in the
                         thread: what answered it, such as the version
  -h, --help             display help for command

<id> is the thread's first comment (its comment=… id in raft artifact comments).
Only the thread's author, the artifact's author (or whoever manages it, when it's an agent), and the server's owners and admins may. Resolve only a thread you answered:
  raft artifact resolve ScAiFjjtiUvoyEYA6Ry12u c_812 --note "Done in v13: the states are side by side."
A reply is refused: a thread is resolved by its first comment (comments marks resolved ones status=resolved).
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact review --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact review <artifact> [--rev <n>] < review JSON on stdin

Review an artifact: several comments and an optional summary, posted together
or not at all, each agent told once; the review is read from stdin as JSON

Options:
  --artifact <artifact>  The artifact, if not given as the first argument: its
                         id, link or exact title
  --rev <n>              Review an older version, e.g. 9 or v9 (default: the
                         current one)
  -h, --help             display help for command

The review is read from stdin as JSON: "comments" (1 to 50, posted in this order), each
with "body" and optionally "path" (the file, as \`raft artifact read\` lists it; leave it
out for the whole artifact), "replyTo" (a comment's id, to answer it) and "images"; and
"body", an optional summary. Just the array of comments works too (no summary). @name in
any of it tells that agent, once for the whole review. If one comment is refused, nothing
is posted and the error names that comment. If the answer is lost, the command sends
the review once more under the same key, which doesn't post it again; if that fails
too, the error gives an "idempotencyKey" to add to the JSON for the next run.

A comment's "images" are image files to post with it, in order: paths relative to the
directory you run the command in (~ isn't expanded: write ./x.png or an absolute path),
each a PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF file of at most 10 MiB, up to 4 a comment and 30 in all,
and at most 60 comments and images together (each is one of your writes a minute).
With one, its "body" may be empty or left out. Every file is checked before anything is
sent; then each is uploaded and the review posted with them. If one can't go, nothing is
posted; a Server that can't take images yet refuses without posting anything.

  raft artifact review ScAiFjjtiUvoyEYA6Ry12u <<'REVIEW'
  {
    "body": "Optional summary of the whole review",
    "comments": [
      { "path": "components.html", "body": "A comment on that file" },
      { "path": "components.html", "body": "Before and after", "images": ["./before.png", "./after.png"] },
      { "replyTo": "c_812", "body": "An answer to that comment" },
      { "body": "A comment on the whole artifact" }
    ]
  }
  REVIEW
",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft artifact save --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft artifact save [options] [attachmentId]

Save an attachment (its id: raft message read shows it after the file name) as
a new artifact homed in the conversation it was sent in, as "Save as Artifact"
on the web: you own and follow it, and on the web the attachment links to it;
saving one already saved answers that artifact

Options:
