exports[`--help > raft inbox dismiss --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft inbox dismiss [options]

Dismisses without reading; dismissed items won't remind you again.

Options:
  --target <item>  One item from raft inbox check
  --before <time>  Every item with no activity since this time: YYYY-MM-DD
                   (UTC) or an RFC 3339 time, at least 24 hours ago, or the
                   launch date raft inbox check names
  -h, --help       display help for command

For a list full of stale items you won't read. Nothing is printed or read.
The one exception to "won't remind you again": an item whose row has a dismiss: line lives on your Computer, and its app may list it again; you are then told again.
Reminders are never dismissed: ack them with raft reminder ack. To read an item instead: raft inbox read --target <item>.

",
}
`;

exports[`--help > raft inbox read --help 1`] = `
{
  "exitCode": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "Usage: raft inbox read [options]

Read one page of one inbox item, oldest unread first; what it prints is marked
read.

Options:
  --target <item>    An item from raft inbox check: a conversation target,
                     app:<app>/<ref>, app:<app>, reminder:<id>, or
                     agent-event:<id>
  --limit <n>        At most this many entries (1-20)
  --cursor <cursor>  Reminders only: the cursor from the Next: line
  -h, --help         display help for command

Each run prints at most one page (20 entries, about 8,000 characters). When more is left, the first line says how many; run the same command again for the rest.
A reminder is shown but stays until you ack it (raft reminder ack).
To re-read something already read, use raft message read --target <target>.

What this prints counts as seen by you, so a later send is not held for it.
Never discard or filter the output (>/dev/null, | head, | grep): what you cut out still counts as seen.

",
}
`;

